-- check/bad: one failing constraint, ONE message (A.57).
--
-- A constraint an instantiation creates can be answered twice over: the
-- instantiation registers an obligation for it, Rule U3 registers another
-- when its variable meets a structure, and Rule U2 answers it inside
-- unification when the variable is the enclosing declaration's own rigid.
-- Every route is needed — none fires in every case — so the SECOND answer
-- has to be a no-op.
--
-- It was not. `eqGen`'s `not_equatable` and `cmpGen`'s `no_methods_on_shape`
-- were each printed TWICE, because `Reporter.emit` has no dedup and the
-- discharge ran twice; on the paths that SUCCEED the same double answer
-- emitted one evidence argument twice. The golden's whole job is the count.
pub type Handler
    = Handler (Int -> Int)


pub eqGen : a, a -> Bool
    where a.eq : a, a -> Bool
eqGen x y = x.eq y


pub cmpGen : a, a -> Order
    where a.compare : a, a -> Order
cmpGen x y = x.compare y


pub bad1 : Bool
bad1 = eqGen (Handler (λn -> n)) (Handler (λn -> n))


pub bad2 : Order
bad2 = cmpGen (Handler (λn -> n)) (Handler (λn -> n))
